          "content": "\n\nNo, not really. On iPhone you can't just sideload an APK-equivalent file. Here's the situation:\n\n**Android** — You can install any `.apk` by just enabling \"Install from unknown sources.\" Done.\n\n**iPhone** — Apple locks this down. Your options:\n\n1. **TestFlight** — Apple's official beta testing tool. You'd need a Mac, Xcode, an Apple Developer account ($99/year), build an `.ipa`, upload to App Store Connect, then invite people via TestFlight. Works for up to 10,000 testers.\n\n2. **AltStore/Sideloadly** — Third-party tools that let you sideload `.ipa` files using a free Apple ID, but apps expire every 7 days and need re-signing. Annoying.\n\n3. **Web app (PWA)** — A website that works like an app. No app store needed, works on both Android and iPhone. You'd lose the widget and notifications, but the \"what's playing now\" and lineup pages would work fine. This is probably the best option if you want to share with iPhone friends.\n\nWant me to build a quick PWA version of the festival app that anyone can open from a browser link? It would work on any phone without installing anything."
